---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import Breadcrumbs from "../../components/seo/Breadcrumbs.astro";
import { siteOrigin } from "../../config/site";

const title = "Which Countries Don't Observe Daylight Saving Time?";
const description =
  "Most countries do not change their clocks. See which places stay on standard time in 2026, which stopped daylight saving, and where the rule depends on the region.";

const checked = "5 November 2026";

const faqs = [
  {
    question: "Which countries don't observe daylight saving time?",
    answer:
      "Most countries do not. In the 2026 observance lists checked for this page, China, Japan, India, Russia, Turkey, Brazil, and most of Africa and the Gulf do not change clocks. The United States and Europe still do, except for places such as Hawaii, most of Arizona, and Iceland. In Canada, Ontario, Quebec, and the Atlantic provinces still change. Yukon, much of Saskatchewan, and, since 1 November 2026, most of British Columbia, Alberta, and Manitoba do not.",
  },
    {
      question: "Which countries stopped changing clocks?",
      answer:
        "Pew Research Center lists Russia, Turkey, Iran, Jordan, and Syria among the places that ended seasonal clock changes in the previous decade. Its correction of 15 July 2026 says Brazil stopped in 2019. Argentina is not listed as changing clocks in 2026. In tzdb-2026e, the main zones for British Columbia, Alberta, and Manitoba have no further daylight-saving rule after 1 November 2026.",
    },
    {
      question: "Where does daylight saving vary by region?",
      answer:
        "It varies in the United States, Canada, Mexico, Australia, and Chile. Hawaii and most of Arizona do not change clocks. The Navajo Nation does. Queensland, Western Australia, and the Northern Territory do not change. Sydney does.",
    },
    {
      question: "How many countries don't observe daylight saving time?",
    answer:
      "There is no single official count. Pew Research Center's 2023 analysis of 193 UN member states, corrected on 15 July 2026, said only about a third practice daylight saving or another regular clock change. timeanddate.com's 2026 list, which includes territories and splits some countries, reports 178 places with no daylight saving and 70 where at least one location changes clocks. Those figures count different things, so this page does not turn either number into the number of countries.",
  },
  {
    question: "Does India observe daylight saving time?",
    answer:
      "No. The 2026 observance list shows no clock change for India. India Standard Time stays at UTC+5:30 all year.",
  },
  {
    question: "Which US states don't observe daylight saving time?",
    answer:
      "Hawaii does not. Most of Arizona does not, but the Navajo Nation does, which is why the 2026 list says most of Arizona rather than all of it. Puerto Rico, the US Virgin Islands, Guam, American Samoa, and the Northern Mariana Islands are also listed as not changing clocks in 2026.",
  },
  {
    question: "Does Europe still use daylight saving time?",
    answer:
      "Yes. The United Kingdom, Ireland, and European Union countries are listed as changing clocks in 2026, on 29 March and 25 October. Iceland is not. The UK, Portugal, and Ireland stay an hour behind Spain, France, and Germany in both winter and summer.",
  },
];

const structuredData = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "WebPage",
      name: title,
      description,
      url: new URL("/daylight-saving/", siteOrigin).toString(),
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: faqs.map((item) => ({
        "@type": "Question",
        name: item.question,
        acceptedAnswer: { "@type": "Answer", text: item.answer },
      })),
    },
  ],
};
---

<Layout title={title} description={description} structuredData={structuredData}>
  <article class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 py-10 sm:px-6">
    <Breadcrumbs
      items={[
        { label: "Home", href: "/" },
        { label: "Daylight saving time", href: "/daylight-saving/" },
      ]}
    />
    <h1 class="text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-paper-100">
      Which countries don't observe daylight saving time?
    </h1>
    <p class="mt-3 text-lg text-paper-100">
      Most countries do not change their clocks for summer. China, Japan, India, Russia, Turkey,
      Brazil, and most of Africa and the Gulf stay on the same offset all year. The United States
      and Europe still change clocks, with exceptions such as Hawaii, most of Arizona, and Iceland.
      In Canada, Ontario, Quebec, and the Atlantic provinces still change. Yukon, much of
      Saskatchewan, and, since 1 November 2026, most of British Columbia, Alberta, and Manitoba do not.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-3 text-paper-300">
      <a href="#where" class="underline hover:text-paper-100">Countries that stopped changing clocks</a>{" "}
      include Russia, Turkey, Iran, Jordan, and Syria. Pew Research Center lists them among the
      places that ended seasonal clock changes in the previous decade. Its correction of 15 July
      2026 says Brazil stopped in 2019. Argentina is not listed as changing clocks in 2026. In
      tzdb-2026e, the main zones for British Columbia, Alberta, and Manitoba have no further
      daylight-saving rule after 1 November 2026.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-3 text-paper-300">
      <a href="#varies" class="underline hover:text-paper-100">Daylight saving varies by region</a>{" "}
      in the United States, Canada, Mexico, Australia, and Chile. Hawaii and most of Arizona do not
      change clocks. The Navajo Nation does. Queensland, Western Australia, and the Northern
      Territory do not change. Sydney does.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-3 text-paper-400">
      The table splits that answer into places that do not change clocks, places that stopped, and
      places where the rule is not the same everywhere. It was checked on {checked}. A government
      can change the law after that, so the
      <a href="/" class="underline hover:text-paper-100">world clock</a>
      is the check for a particular date. This page does not publish one number for "how many
      countries," because the published counts do not count the same things.
    </p>

    <nav aria-label="On this page" class="mt-6 rounded-lg border border-navy-700 bg-navy-800/40 p-4 text-sm">
      <p class="font-medium text-paper-200">On this page</p>
      <ul class="mt-2 grid gap-1 text-paper-400 sm:grid-cols-2">
        <li><a class="hover:text-paper-100 hover:underline" href="#where">Where clocks do not change</a></li>
        <li><a class="hover:text-paper-100 hover:underline" href="#varies">Where the rule varies</a></li>
        <li><a class="hover:text-paper-100 hover:underline" href="#still">Where clocks still change</a></li>
        <li><a class="hover:text-paper-100 hover:underline" href="#count">Why there is no single count</a></li>
        <li><a class="hover:text-paper-100 hover:underline" href="#sources">Sources and what is uncertain</a></li>
        <li><a class="hover:text-paper-100 hover:underline" href="#questions">Common questions</a></li>
      </ul>
    </nav>

    <section class="mt-8">
      <h2 id="where" class="text-xl font-semibold text-paper-100">Where clocks do not change</h2>
      <p class="mt-2 text-paper-300">
        "Does not change" means the 2026 observance list shows no daylight-saving start or end for
        that place. Offsets such as UTC+5:30 are the standard offset, not a seasonal one.
      </p>
      <div class="mt-4 overflow-x-auto">
        <table class="w-full text-left text-sm text-paper-300">
          <caption class="sr-only">Places that do not change clocks, or that stopped changing them</caption>
          <thead>
            <tr class="border-b border-navy-700 text-paper-400">
              <th scope="col" class="py-2 pr-4 font-medium">Place</th>
              <th scope="col" class="py-2 pr-4 font-medium">2026 pattern</th>
              <th scope="col" class="py-2 font-medium">What to know</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr class="border-b border-navy-800 align-top">
              <th scope="row" class="py-3 pr-4 font-medium text-paper-100">China, Japan, South Korea, Taiwan, Hong Kong, Singapore</th>
              <td class="py-3 pr-4">Does not change</td>
              <td class="py-3">Japan is UTC+9. China uses one national time, UTC+8, across the whole country.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-navy-800 align-top">
              <th scope="row" class="py-3 pr-4 font-medium text-paper-100">India, Sri Lanka, Nepal, Pakistan, Bangladesh</th>
              <td class="py-3 pr-4">Does not change</td>
              <td class="py-3">India and Sri Lanka are UTC+5:30. Nepal is UTC+5:45. Pakistan is UTC+5. Bangladesh is UTC+6.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-navy-800 align-top">
              <th scope="row" class="py-3 pr-4 font-medium text-paper-100">Indonesia, Malaysia, Thailand, Vietnam, the Philippines</th>
              <td class="py-3 pr-4">Does not change</td>
              <td class="py-3">Indonesia has more than one standard offset. None of those zones is listed as changing clocks in 2026.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-navy-800 align-top">
              <th scope="row" class="py-3 pr-4 font-medium text-paper-100">United Arab Emirates, Saudi Arabia, Qatar, Kuwait, Bahrain, Oman</th>
              <td class="py-3 pr-4">Does not change</td>
              <td class="py-3">Dubai and Riyadh do not spring forward.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-navy-800 align-top">
              <th scope="row" class="py-3 pr-4 font-medium text-paper-100">Russia, Turkey, Iran, Jordan, Syria</th>
              <td class="py-3 pr-4">Stopped</td>
              <td class="py-3">No clock change is listed for 2026. Pew lists Russia, Turkey, Iran, Jordan, and Syria among the places that ended seasonal clock changes in the previous decade.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-navy-800 align-top">
              <th scope="row" class="py-3 pr-4 font-medium text-paper-100">Brazil and Argentina</th>
              <td class="py-3 pr-4">Stopped</td>
              <td class="py-3">Neither is listed as changing clocks in 2026. Brazil's last daylight-saving period was in 2019.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="align-top">
              <th scope="row" class="py-3 pr-4 font-medium text-paper-100">Most of Africa</th>
              <td class="py-3 pr-4">Does not change</td>
              <td class="py-3">Nigeria, Kenya, South Africa, Ghana, Ethiopia, and Tanzania are examples with no 2026 clock change. Egypt and Morocco are not in this group. See the next section.</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-8">
      <h2 id="varies" class="text-xl font-semibold text-paper-100">Where the rule varies</h2>
      <p class="mt-2 text-paper-300">
        A country name is not enough when part of the country changes clocks and part does not.
      </p>
      <div class="mt-4 overflow-x-auto">
        <table class="w-full text-left text-sm text-paper-300">
          <caption class="sr-only">Places where daylight saving depends on the region</caption>
          <thead>
            <tr class="border-b border-navy-700 text-paper-400">
              <th scope="col" class="py-2 pr-4 font-medium">Place</th>
              <th scope="col" class="py-2 pr-4 font-medium">2026 pattern</th>
              <th scope="col" class="py-2 font-medium">What to know</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr class="border-b border-navy-800 align-top">
              <th scope="row" class="py-3 pr-4 font-medium text-paper-100">United States</th>
              <td class="py-3 pr-4">Varies</td>
              <td class="py-3">Most states change on 8 March and 1 November 2026. Hawaii does not. Most of Arizona does not. The Navajo Nation does, so the IANA database points Navajo locations at America/Denver rather than America/Phoenix. Puerto Rico, the US Virgin Islands, Guam, American Samoa, and the Northern Mariana Islands do not change.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-navy-800 align-top">
              <th scope="row" class="py-3 pr-4 font-medium text-paper-100">Canada</th>
              <td class="py-3 pr-4">Varies</td>
              <td class="py-3">Ontario, Quebec, and the Atlantic provinces still change on the US dates. In tzdb-2026e, the main zones for British Columbia, Alberta, and Manitoba make the 1 November 2026 change and then have no further daylight-saving rule: British Columbia stays on UTC−7, Alberta on UTC−6, and Manitoba on UTC−5. Yukon has not changed clocks since 2020 and stays on UTC−7. Much of Saskatchewan already stays on UTC−6. A Saskatchewan bill received royal assent on 14 May 2026. The 21 September 2026 note in that same file still says the new act takes effect only by a later order, so this page does not treat it as already in force. The 2026 observance list also shows small areas of Nunavut, Quebec, and Ontario staying on standard time.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-navy-800 align-top">
              <th scope="row" class="py-3 pr-4 font-medium text-paper-100">Mexico</th>
              <td class="py-3 pr-4">Varies</td>
              <td class="py-3">The national summer-time rule ended in 2022, so Mexico City does not change. Baja California still follows US Pacific rules. Listed northern border municipalities in Chihuahua, Coahuila, Nuevo León, and Tamaulipas still follow the neighboring US zone. Most of Nuevo León, away from that border list, does not. Sonora does not.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-navy-800 align-top">
              <th scope="row" class="py-3 pr-4 font-medium text-paper-100">Australia</th>
              <td class="py-3 pr-4">Varies</td>
              <td class="py-3">Queensland, Western Australia, and the Northern Territory do not change. The rest of the country does, on a southern-hemisphere summer. In that summer, <a href="/time/sydney/" class="underline hover:text-paper-100">Sydney</a> is one hour ahead of Brisbane. In the Australian winter, Sydney and Brisbane match. Sydney's names for those offsets, AEST and AEDT, are on the Sydney time zone page.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-navy-800 align-top">
              <th scope="row" class="py-3 pr-4 font-medium text-paper-100">Chile</th>
              <td class="py-3 pr-4">Varies</td>
              <td class="py-3">Most of Chile changes clocks for the southern summer. The 2026 overview also lists some Chilean locations as not changing. It does not name them in the summary row, so this page does not guess which ones.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="align-top">
              <th scope="row" class="py-3 pr-4 font-medium text-paper-100">Egypt and Morocco</th>
              <td class="py-3 pr-4">Still changes, on different schedules</td>
              <td class="py-3">Egypt is listed as changing clocks from 24 April to 30 October 2026. Morocco is listed with a 2026 clock change that is not one ordinary summer period. Do not group either country with the African countries that stay on standard time.</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-8">
      <h2 id="still" class="text-xl font-semibold text-paper-100">Where clocks still change</h2>
      <p class="mt-2 text-paper-300">
        The United Kingdom, Ireland, and European Union countries are listed as moving clocks on
        29 March 2026 and back on 25 October 2026. Iceland does not. The UK, Portugal, and Ireland
        stay an hour behind Spain, France, and Germany in both winter and summer, because the
        seasonal change does not close that gap.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-2 text-paper-300">
        New Zealand is listed as changing clocks for the southern summer. The weeks when two
        places that both change clocks are a different number of hours apart are explained on the
        <a href="/time-difference/" class="underline hover:text-paper-100">time difference</a>
        page. Sample dates for a few cities are on
        <a href="/time/" class="underline hover:text-paper-100">time in major cities</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-8">
      <h2 id="count" class="text-xl font-semibold text-paper-100">Why there is no single count</h2>
      <p class="mt-2 text-paper-300">
        Pew Research Center looked at the 193 UN member states and, in an article dated 26 October
        2023 and corrected on 15 July 2026, said only about a third practice daylight saving or
        another regular clock change. The correction removed Brazil, which stopped in 2019. That
        "about a third" is not a list you can add up from this page.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-2 text-paper-300">
        timeanddate.com's 2026 table counts a wider set, including territories, and splits some
        countries. It reports 178 places with no daylight saving at all and 70 where at least one
        location observes it. Those 178 places are not 178 countries. A country that changes clocks
        only on its northern border is not in the same bucket as a country that never changes them.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-2 text-paper-300">
        Use the tables above for the places they name. For a city they do not name, search it on
        the world clock rather than extending either published count.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-8">
      <h2 id="sources" class="text-xl font-semibold text-paper-100">Sources and what is uncertain</h2>
      <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-paper-600">Checked {checked}.</p>
      <ul class="mt-3 list-disc space-y-2 pl-5 text-paper-300">
        <li>
          <a class="underline hover:text-paper-100" href="https://www.timeanddate.com/time/dst/2026.html" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">timeanddate.com, Daylight Saving Time Around the World 2026</a>.
          That site says the list may change when a country announces a rule with little notice.
        </li>
        <li>
          <a class="underline hover:text-paper-100" href="https://www.pewresearch.org/short-reads/2023/10/26/most-countries-dont-observe-daylight-saving-time/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">Pew Research Center, 26 October 2023</a>,
          correction 15 July 2026. Used for the UN-member framing, for countries that stopped, and for the "about a third" statement. Not used as a 2026 city-by-city list.
        </li>
        <li>
          <a class="underline hover:text-paper-100" href="https://data.iana.org/time-zones/tzdb-2026e/northamerica" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">IANA time zone database, tzdb-2026e, northamerica</a>.
          Used for the Navajo Nation, the Mexico border municipalities, and the Saskatchewan bill that is not yet in force. The live clocks on this site use your browser's copy of the same database, which can lag a very new law.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p class="mt-3 text-paper-300">
        On 10 November 2026 the Canada lines were compared again with tzdb-2026e. timeanddate.com's
        2026 page could not be retrieved that day, so its 178 and 70 counts, and the Egypt,
        Morocco, and Chile lines, were not re-read. Not verified here: the names of the Chilean
        locations that do not change clocks in 2026, and whether every small Canadian border town
        matches the provincial summary. The US Department of Transportation daylight-saving page
        was not retrieved during this check, so the Hawaii and Arizona lines rely on the 2026
        observance list and the IANA notes rather than a quotation from that department.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-3 text-paper-300">
        How a world clock chooses an offset is explained in the
        <a href="/guide/#dst" class="underline hover:text-paper-100">time zone guide</a>.
        Every country in the dataset is listed on
        <a href="/clocks/" class="underline hover:text-paper-100">world clocks</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-8">
      <h2 id="questions" class="text-xl font-semibold text-paper-100">Common questions</h2>
      <dl class="mt-4 space-y-5">
        {
          faqs.map((item) => (
            <div>
              <dt class="font-semibold text-paper-100">{item.question}</dt>
              <dd class="mt-1 text-paper-300">{item.answer}</dd>
            </div>
          ))
        }
      </dl>
    </section>
  </article>
</Layout>
